# `SELECT ... FROM` after an operator: a rewrite would drop `where`, so only the help is given.
mode: syntax
---- query
t | where x > 1 | SELECT a FROM t
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..34
  PIPELINE@0..33
    NAME_REF@0..1
      IDENT@0..1 "t"
    WHITESPACE@1..2 " "
    PIPE@2..3 "|"
    WHITESPACE@3..4 " "
    WHERE_OP@4..15
      IDENT@4..9 "where"
      WHITESPACE@9..10 " "
      BIN_EXPR@10..15
        NAME_REF@10..11
          IDENT@10..11 "x"
        WHITESPACE@11..12 " "
        GT@12..13 ">"
        WHITESPACE@13..14 " "
        LITERAL@14..15
          LONG@14..15 "1"
    WHITESPACE@15..16 " "
    PIPE@16..17 "|"
    WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
    ERROR@18..33
      IDENT@18..24 "SELECT"
      WHITESPACE@24..25 " "
      IDENT@25..26 "a"
      WHITESPACE@26..27 " "
      IDENT@27..31 "FROM"
      WHITESPACE@31..32 " "
      IDENT@32..33 "t"
  WHITESPACE@33..34 "\n"

error[DS0105]: `SELECT ... FROM` is SQL, not DataScript
  --> query:1:19
  |
1 | t | where x > 1 | SELECT a FROM t
  |                   ^^^^^^   ---- in DataScript the table comes first
  |                   |
  |                   this is SQL
  |
  = help: start with the table and pipe it into operators: `'orders.csv' | where amount > 10 | project customerId, amount`
